00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Welcome to the DDPS
seminar, everyone. It is an

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.150
honor to your host, Paul
Atzberger, who is currently a

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.630
professor at the Department of
Mathematics of University of

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.100
California, Santa Barbara.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.640
All right, thank you, like thank
the organizers for this

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.760
opportunity to talk today, I
thought I would share a little

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.390
bit of work that we've been
doing on leveraging recent

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.780
developments in generative
modeling, and particular machine

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.450
learning approaches that have
been emerging, especially in the

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.500
deep learning community, for the
purposes of developing

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.740
multiscale models, reductions
and other types of

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.570
representations for nonlinear
dynamical systems have larger

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.150
scientific simulations, you
know, so fluid mechanics,

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.120
materials, modeling, and so on.
So I'm going to talk about a

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.330
rather sort of small piece of
the work that we're doing. And

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.330
this is relatively recent work.
And I'd be very happy to

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.380
continue discussions offline
about various directions and

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.790
applications. So just to set a
little bit, the context in which

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:03.450
I'm motivated in developing
these data driven methods, is,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.920
what we're really after are ways
that we can utilize observations

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.730
either taken from experiment or
higher fidelity computational

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:16.170
simulations, and really use data
driven perspectives to

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.020
essentially learn
representations, which we can

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.100
use as a simulator either to
predict future behaviors of the

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.850
system, or in the context of a
much larger calculation where

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.370
perhaps the part of the model
that we're interested in

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.830
replacing is very
computationally expensive to

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.220
compute at a high level of
detail. But for the purposes of

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.460
larger scale phenomena that
we're interested in a much sort

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.200
of coarsened or simplified model
would potentially do the trick.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.990
But it might be very difficult
to arrive at that through kind

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.950
of traditional asymptotic
techniques. And so what I'm also

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.680
interested in is in doing this
in the context, not only of

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.920
deterministic systems, but
stochastic systems. And that's

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.940
primarily what I'll talk about
today, I feel like again, to

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.040
build some context, I need to
say a little bit about

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.530
techniques that are out there
and have been successfully

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.630
applied for this type of
problem. So they can kind of

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.080
compare a little bit and
contrast some of the objectives

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.440
of the techniques that we're
developing. And so there's been

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.140
a lot of work done over the
years, of course, a data driven

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.430
modeling of dynamics, probably
one of the most notable one is

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.100
this proper orthogonal
decomposition. This is

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.940
essentially a form of principal
component analysis, where the

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.420
basic strategy to build up your
dynamical model, I'm here, I'm

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.120
just showing a fluid mechanics
model on the upper right would

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.030
be to look at the modes in the
system, essentially, that vary

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.890
the most. So essentially, you
would take your flow field

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.010
create this very high
dimensional point cloud, and

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.470
effectively you're doing
principal component and on that

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.200
a really nice development in the
direction of incorporating

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.590
dynamics within this kind of
linear frameworks is the dynamic

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.600
mode decomposition, introduced
by Schmid in 2010. And lots of

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.780
developments by by various
groups and communities,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.920
including Bronto, and Coots, in
various directions. And again,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.340
this is a linear based model.
Again, that's there's a lot of

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.160
advantages. Having linear base
models, they're much more

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.130
straightforward to build theory
for and, you know, potentially

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.770
computationally efficient, that
more efficient than training,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.040
let's say, deep neural networks.
And so the basic idea in things

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.100
like dynamic mode decomposition
is you're basically going to

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.410
take dynamical information into
account by saying, well, which

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.250
modes are sort of most
indicative of that prediction of

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.590
what's going to happen in the
future over some set of

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.470
snapshots. So you're basically
looking for, again, the sort of

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.890
best linear model and kind of
the best basis in order to try

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.610
to align your your linear
predictor with the underlying

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.520
data observations. There has
also been really nice work done

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:45.990
on using symbolic approaches,
essentially, l one regression

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.830
things like the lawsuit combined
with the symbolic dictionaries,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.990
and that's a way that you can
overcome some of the issues that

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.810
I mentioned in these previous
linear models. And again, lots

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.750
of successes and nice
applications of these

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.050
techniques. And but you know,
this has the drawback that you

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.630
do need an sufficiently
expressive library so that you

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.840
can have coverage over the space
of potential dynamic

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.840
representations that are
relevant for your system. So my

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.540
main point of mentioning these
approaches, I just want to give

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.970
kind of some context of what's
out there. And it kind of just

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.630
helps set a little bit, the the
overarching environment in which

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.330
we're trying to develop these
data driven techniques, I'm also

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.910
gonna incorporate noise. So
they'll also be a generative

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.940
component that I'll be talking
about in detail. And so the

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.410
objective is, try to not just
recapitulate the behavior that

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.930
you're seeing in the training
set, but really learn for

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.350
intrinsic model, so that you can
make either longer term

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.350
predictions, or ideally, in the
context of a lot of these

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.200
scientific computing
applications, be able to replace

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.960
one of your more expensive
models with one that's much

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.830
cheaper by a longtime simulator.
And so that poses a lot of

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:53.040
challenges from the data driven
modeling perspective, because,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.760
you know, depending on how you
go about this, your model class

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.340
might not have certain intrinsic
properties that we're used to

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.560
when we design into our
numerical methods, you know,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.740
such as things like stability,
which obviously be very

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.890
important for longtime
prediction. Also, another

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.430
challenge is incorporating prior
knowledge to the extent that we

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.130
can, we often know a lot about,
you know, the physics of the

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.610
system or other mathematical
structure in a system. And so

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.270
we'd also like ways of including
that as inductive biases in our

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.600
data driven methods. So we'll
talk a little bit about where I

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.940
think there are opportunities to
do that as well, in some of

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.640
these nonlinear approximation
approaches, as I said, these

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.730
developments in the neural
networks community in particular

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.710
generative models, and I'll kind
of put it in a little bit

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:38.790
larger, probabilistic context.
And and there's a lot of

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.900
interesting directions, I think
that this can be pursued for for

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.090
a lot of the applications that I
showed on the previous slides.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.290
But going beyond, again, that
sort of linear approximations to

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:52.650
that incorporate various types
of nonlinear approximation

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.800
classes. So as to motivate the
examples, and I'll come back and

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.680
I'll give some results for
these. One of which is, you

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.070
know, basically systems
identification, or extracting

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.440
state information, which is
important in many different

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.460
problems, including things like
control. And the objective here

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.000
basically, is to learn key
parameters, from observations of

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.000
the dynamics. These things go
by, you know, many different

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.790
names, depending on the
community this. Now what we're

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:19.920
trying to utilize here, as I
showed in the upper right, just

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.530
a little animation, if I'm
trying to learn, for example, in

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:26.010
this Orenstein lindbeck process,
you can think of it intuitively

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.190
from the mechanics point of
view, as a bead spring system,

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.860
where I'm trying to learn
simultaneously, the stiffness,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.940
the damping and the temperature
of the system, you know, what we

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.690
want to do is somehow probe the
dynamics in a way that would be

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.270
indicative of those different
properties. So there's going to

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.160
be an interesting challenge,
whenever you're trying to

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.790
estimate a model from the
dynamics that you may, you have

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.640
to be careful basically, that
you're probing the dynamics on

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.940
the appropriate timescales, and
that you have sufficient

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.740
coverage over the response range
that would be sensitive to those

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.560
particular parameters. But the
overall objective here is to try

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.050
to learn dynamical
representations from

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.610
observations, sample
trajectories. Also in reductions

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.270
and course grading. I'll show an
example where, you know, we're

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.670
trying to learn essentially
unknown force laws. And so how

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.640
that can be done again, for this
general perspective. In this

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.970
case, this would be a
nonparametric estimation problem

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.880
where we want to learn an
unknown force law, for example,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.660
in something like lunch, Yvonne
dynamics, and so problems

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:30.930
analogous to this come up in a
lot of different contexts, in

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:35.100
developing again, reductions, so
that you can simulate things on

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.250
larger timescales to overcome,
for example, sources of

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:41.700
stiffness, or in coarse grained
modeling, of course, which is

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.790
very popular, for example, in
the material sciences community.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:46.710
So some of the interesting
things that you could

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.120
potentially do if you had good
nonlinear estimation methods is,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.330
you know, not have to handcraft
your estimators for things like

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.330
the the force law. So one of the
common ways you might try to go

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:59.310
about doing this is to use
something like maximum

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:01.920
likelihood estimation. But of
course, the immediate challenge

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.940
you run into in the setting of
these dynamic representations is

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.330
that you have to specify
likelihood functions, you have

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.640
to compute those potentially on
very high dimensional

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.570
probability spaces. And so I'm
going to show some alternatives

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:18.660
to that approach. Alright, so I
just wanted to give some of the

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.380
kind of key key motivations here
and context of where we're

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.710
trying to develop these
techniques just to give a, a

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.470
sort of overview of some of the
issues that arise. And I think

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.780
there's a lot of potentially
interesting ways, again, to

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.900
leverage a lot of the recent
developments in the machine

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:37.530
learning community. In the case
of stochastic dynamics, in some

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.680
sense, we're trying to learn an
entire ensemble. And so there'll

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.530
be some interesting issues that
arise there, given the roles

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.920
that noise plays in stochastic
phenomena. And then, of course,

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:51.000
as I said before, we'd like to
do all of this to obtain robust

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.640
representations that are stable
for the purposes of either

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.670
longtime prediction or
simulation. Alright, so I think

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.980
I've said, you know, enough at
the sort of the high level, and

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.630
I'll start getting into some of
the more details of the

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.180
techniques that we're going to
leverage and did further

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:08.640
develop. So if you're not
familiar with generative

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.190
adversarial networks, I'm just
gonna go through really quick,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.520
what these techniques are these
recent developments in the

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.790
machine learning community,
closely related to generative

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.700
modeling statistics. And so what
I'm going to do is just go

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.400
through perspective on
generative adversarial networks.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:29.340
This is basically a way for us
to train a simulator or an

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.820
implicit generative model, that
is this function that I'm

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:38.370
showing here, which is g of z.
And g of z basically has some

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:42.000
noise source. And what it's
going to do is generate a sort

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.020
of collection of samples. And
the objective here is to take

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:50.160
our training data and take our
implicit generator and have some

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:53.820
way of comparing the samples
that we get from the the

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.720
training set with the samples
that were produced by the

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.450
generator. Now as I mentioned
before, you know One way that

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.940
this is traditionally be done,
if you know a lot analytically

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.700
about the generator is you could
use things like maximum

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.850
likelihood estimation. But if
you do that, right, this

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.730
requires the specification of
likelihood functions requires

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.750
you to go and perform those
calculations during the training

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.680
loop, which could potentially be
quite quite challenging. The

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.370
appeal of doing this adversarial
training approach is instead of

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:27.060
relying on a sort of pre
specified likelihood function,

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.850
what we're going to do is
simultaneously train another

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.970
model. This could potentially as
well be a neural network, but

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.730
can be taken from any model
class you like. And I'll show a

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.880
little bit about that in a
minute. And basically, the job

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:43.230
of this discriminator is to
characterize how closely the

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:47.970
samples that were produced by
the generator differ. And so the

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.120
discriminator, you can think of
as playing a role analogous to a

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.320
classifier. And if I have this
collection of samples, its job

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:57.270
is to say, Well, did those
samples come from the training

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.850
set or the generator, if they
can distinguish that readily,

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.240
then your you know, your
generator needs to be improved,

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.330
you can think of this as kind of
like a two player game, where if

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.570
I had a classifier loss, for
example, the discriminator would

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:12.990
be aiming to minimize the loss,
while the generator would be

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:16.770
aiming to maximize that loss for
the discriminator. And so by

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.080
making different choices of the
discriminator, we can actually

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.820
realize different divergences
and metrics for comparing the

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.750
empirical measures of the
samples of generator with with

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:30.510
that of the training data
distribution. Alright, so that's

